1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,477
This is MATH 239, video 11. The topics are subsets with restrictions, and the proof of inclusion exclusion.

2
00:00:07,927 --> 00:00:20,092
After this video, you can change a subset into a composition and back, and find generating series of subsets with gap conditions. You can solve assignment 3, question 1. And you can prove inclusion exclusion.

3
00:00:20,542 --> 00:00:35,867
Here is the problem of Example 2.24. r sub n is the number of subsets of 1 to n with no two consecutive numbers. On the left are all 16 subsets of 1 to 4. A filled cell is an element of the subset.

4
00:00:36,317 --> 00:00:42,633
Mark the subsets that contain two consecutive numbers, such as 2 and 3. They are red.

5
00:00:43,083 --> 00:00:46,380
8 subsets are bad. So r sub 4 is 8.

6
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:56,164
We want the generating series R of x. The tool of this section changes each subset into a composition. Then the lemmas for compositions apply.

7
00:00:56,614 --> 00:01:12,677
The set U contains the pairs n, A, where A is a subset of 1 to n. We keep n in the pair, because the same set A is a subset of many sets 1 to n. Our example is 7 and the set 2, 3, 6.

8
00:01:13,127 --> 00:01:22,137
Draw n plus 1 cells in a row. Here n is 7, so there are 8 cells. Between the cells are 7 boundaries, numbered 1 to 7.

9
00:01:22,587 --> 00:01:27,463
The set A tells us where to cut. A contains 2, so cut at boundary 2.

10
00:01:27,913 --> 00:01:31,457
A also contains 3 and 6. Cut there too.

11
00:01:31,907 --> 00:01:45,839
The cuts make 4 parts: 2, 1, 3, 2. This is the composition of the pair. In the notes, c sub i equals a sub i minus a sub i minus 1, with end points 0 and n plus 1.

12
00:01:46,289 --> 00:01:56,041
Now the proof. Put the points a sub 0 to a sub 4 on a number line. They increase strictly, so each gap c sub i is a positive integer.

13
00:01:56,491 --> 00:02:00,717
The first gap goes from 0 to 2. So c sub 1 is 2.

14
00:02:01,167 --> 00:02:17,839
The other gaps are 1, 3 and 2. Add all the gaps. The sum telescopes: each middle point is added once and subtracted once. Only n plus 1 minus 0 stays. So the size of gamma is n plus 1.

15
00:02:18,289 --> 00:02:29,720
A has k elements, and there are k plus 1 gaps. So the length of gamma is the size of A plus 1. The length is at least 1, so gamma is not the empty composition.

16
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:38,436
The table in the notes: n is the size of gamma minus 1, and the size of A is the length of gamma minus 1. Remember the minus 1 in the size.

17
00:02:38,886 --> 00:02:45,063
Now go back. Start with a nonempty composition. Here it is 2, 1, 3, 2.

18
00:02:45,513 --> 00:02:50,203
Add the parts from the left. These are the partial sums. The first one is 2.

19
00:02:50,653 --> 00:02:54,972
The next partial sums are 3 and 6. They are the positions of the cuts.

20
00:02:55,422 --> 00:03:04,571
The last partial sum is 8, the size of gamma. It is not a cut, so we drop it. A is the set 2, 3, 6, and n is 8 minus 1, which is 7.

21
00:03:05,021 --> 00:03:15,934
So we get the start pair back. Each map undoes the other, so by Proposition 1.11 both maps are bijections. The details are Exercise 2.18.

22
00:03:16,384 --> 00:03:28,412
Here is the map for all subsets of 1, 2, 3. So n is 3, and each composition has size 4. The empty set has no cut. It gives the composition with one part, 4.

23
00:03:28,862 --> 00:03:37,639
Each set with one element makes one cut. It gives two parts: 1, 3, then 2, 2, then 3, 1.

24
00:03:38,089 --> 00:03:41,015
Each set with two elements gives three parts.

25
00:03:41,465 --> 00:03:48,152
The full set gives 1, 1, 1, 1. These are all 8 compositions of 4, each one time.

26
00:03:48,602 --> 00:03:56,311
Each pair gives size n plus 1, which is at least 1. So no pair maps to the empty composition. That is why we remove it.

27
00:03:56,761 --> 00:04:09,160
Check the bijection with generating series. Give the pair n, A the weight n. There are 2 to the n subsets of 1 to n. So the series of U is 1 over 1 minus 2 x.

28
00:04:09,610 --> 00:04:19,874
Nonempty compositions, by size, have the series x over 1 minus 2 x, from video 10. The second row is the first row moved one place to the right.

29
00:04:20,324 --> 00:04:29,191
So the series of the compositions is x times the series of U, because the size is n plus 1. We use this shift in every problem of this section.

30
00:04:29,641 --> 00:04:42,226
Now use the bijection for Example 2.24. Take n equals 7 and A equals 2, 4, 7. The parts are 2, 2, 3, 1. A middle part is the gap between two elements of A.

31
00:04:42,676 --> 00:04:57,119
Now take A equals 2, 3, 6. It contains 2 and 3. Their gap is a middle part equal to 1, in red. Two consecutive elements of A give a middle part equal to 1, and the converse is also true.

32
00:04:57,569 --> 00:05:08,529
So the pair is in R exactly when every middle part is at least 2. The first part is 1 when 1 is in A. The last part is 1 when n is in A. Both are allowed.

33
00:05:08,979 --> 00:05:20,665
Now write the solution in the course form. R sub n is the set of pairs n, A with no two consecutive numbers in A. R is the union over all n, and the weight of a pair is n.

34
00:05:21,115 --> 00:05:24,923
M is the set of compositions that correspond to the pairs in R.

35
00:05:25,373 --> 00:05:34,522
The size of gamma is n plus 1. So M of x equals x times R of x. We find M of x first, and then divide by x.

36
00:05:34,972 --> 00:05:47,325
Split M by the number of parts, l. For l equals 1, there is one part, of any positive size. Its series is x plus x squared plus and so on, which is x over 1 minus x.

37
00:05:47,775 --> 00:05:59,199
For l at least 2, there is a first part, l minus 2 middle parts, and a last part. A middle part is at least 2. Its series is x squared over 1 minus x.

38
00:05:59,649 --> 00:06:08,241
By the Product Lemma, the series for l parts is the product of the part series. That is x to the 2 l minus 2, over 1 minus x to the l.

39
00:06:08,691 --> 00:06:17,127
The lengths are disjoint cases. So by the Sum Lemma, x R of x is the l equals 1 term, plus the sum over l at least 2.

40
00:06:17,577 --> 00:06:30,162
Put j equals l minus 2. What is left is a geometric series in x squared over 1 minus x. The table shows its powers. That series has no constant term, so each column has finitely many terms.

41
00:06:30,612 --> 00:06:35,395
Sum the geometric series. It is 1 over 1 minus x squared over 1 minus x.

42
00:06:35,845 --> 00:06:51,170
Bring both terms to the denominator 1 minus x, times 1 minus x minus x squared. The numerators add to x minus x cubed. Cancel the factor 1 minus x. We get x plus x squared over 1 minus x minus x squared.

43
00:06:51,620 --> 00:07:00,444
Divide by x. R of x equals 1 plus x, over 1 minus x minus x squared. This is the answer of Example 2.24.

44
00:07:00,894 --> 00:07:10,089
Expand the answer as a power series. The coefficients are 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34.

45
00:07:10,539 --> 00:07:16,158
Direct counts for n up to 7 agree. For n equals 4 we get 8, as on the first slide.

46
00:07:16,608 --> 00:07:28,218
Each number is the sum of the two numbers before it. These are the Fibonacci numbers. Chapter 4 shows how a denominator such as 1 minus x minus x squared gives such a rule.

47
00:07:28,668 --> 00:07:35,170
Here is the method for any condition on the gaps. Step 1: write the condition on the set A.

48
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:47,601
Step 2: change it into a condition on the parts. A middle part is the gap between two elements. The first part is a sub 1. The last part is n plus 1 minus a sub k.

49
00:07:48,051 --> 00:07:51,720
Step 3: for each length l, use the Product Lemma.

50
00:07:52,170 --> 00:08:02,340
Step 4: add over all lengths with the Sum Lemma. When only the middle parts have a condition, you always get this form, with P of x the series of one middle part.

51
00:08:02,790 --> 00:08:09,060
Step 5: divide by x, because the size is n plus 1. Then check the first few coefficients by hand.

52
00:08:09,510 --> 00:08:23,302
Now an exercise from the notes. Pause the video and try it. Consecutive elements must differ by at most 2. 1, 3, 4 is allowed. 1, 4 is not, because its gap is 3.

53
00:08:23,752 --> 00:08:34,710
Consecutive elements give the middle parts. So a middle part is 1 or 2, and its series P of x is x plus x squared. The first and last parts can be any positive integer.

54
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:37,617
Put P into the formula of the method.

55
00:08:38,067 --> 00:08:41,550
Divide by x, and bring the terms to one denominator.

56
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:56,210
The numerator is 1 minus x plus x cubed. The coefficients are 1, 2, 4, 8, 15, 27. For n equals 4, only 1, 4 fails, so the count is 15, which agrees.

57
00:08:56,660 --> 00:09:13,797
Now assignment 3, question 1. It also uses gaps. Pause the video and try it. S is the set of triples a, b, c of natural numbers with a at most b at most c. The weight is a plus b plus c. The figure shows 1, 3, 4.

58
00:09:14,247 --> 00:09:21,530
Cut each bar at the heights a and b. The bottom piece has height x equals a. It is in all three bars.

59
00:09:21,980 --> 00:09:27,441
The middle piece has height y equals b minus a. It is in the bars of b and c.

60
00:09:27,891 --> 00:09:34,811
The top piece z equals c minus b is only in the bar of c. x, y and z are any natural numbers.

61
00:09:35,261 --> 00:09:42,784
So a plus b plus c equals 3 x plus 2 y plus z. For 1, 3, 4: 3 plus 4 plus 1 is 8.

62
00:09:43,234 --> 00:09:57,305
Here is the posted solution. Let T be N cubed, all triples x, y, z of natural numbers. Give T the weight w prime equals 3 x plus 2 y plus z. On the left are the 4 triples in S of weight 4.

63
00:09:57,755 --> 00:10:06,858
g takes a, b, c to a, b minus a, c minus b. Its inverse f takes partial sums. The question lets you state the inverse without a proof.

64
00:10:07,308 --> 00:10:15,435
g keeps the weight: 3 a plus 2 times b minus a, plus c minus b, is a plus b plus c.

65
00:10:15,885 --> 00:10:23,176
So g is a bijection between the elements of weight n in S and in T, for every n. So the two series are equal.

66
00:10:23,626 --> 00:10:46,195
T is a Cartesian product of three copies of N. The weight is a sum: 3 x, plus 2 y, plus z. So the Product Lemma applies. N with the weight i times m has the series 1 over 1 minus x to the i. The product is 1 over 1 minus x, times 1 minus x squared, times 1 minus x cubed.

67
00:10:46,645 --> 00:10:59,974
Check: the coefficient of x to the 4 is 4, and S has 4 triples of weight 4. In a written answer, define T and w prime, give the bijection and its inverse, show that it keeps the weight, and name the Product Lemma.

68
00:11:00,424 --> 00:11:17,374
Section 2.5 proves inclusion exclusion from video 5. A sub S is the intersection of the sets A sub i for i in S. The size of the union is the sum over nonempty S of minus 1 to the size of S minus 1, times the size of A sub S.

69
00:11:17,824 --> 00:11:25,858
For three sets, first add the sizes of A 1, A 2 and A 3. The numbers show how often each region is counted.

70
00:11:26,308 --> 00:11:32,903
Subtract the three pairwise intersections. Now the pair regions are counted once. The center is counted zero times.

71
00:11:33,353 --> 00:11:39,669
Add the triple intersection. Now every region is counted exactly once. The proof shows this for any m.

72
00:11:40,119 --> 00:11:54,979
The proof needs one lemma. For a nonempty set T, the sum over nonempty subsets S of minus 1 to the size of S minus 1 is 1. The figure shows the 8 subsets of T equals 1, 2, 3.

73
00:11:55,429 --> 00:12:03,046
From the proof of the binomial theorem, the sum over all subsets S of x to the size of S is 1 plus x to the n.

74
00:12:03,496 --> 00:12:17,892
Both sides are polynomials, so put x equals minus 1. The right side is 0 to the n, which is 0 because n is at least 1. In the figure: 1 minus 3 plus 3 minus 1 is 0.

75
00:12:18,342 --> 00:12:28,977
The empty set gives the term 1. Move it out of the sum, and multiply by minus 1. The sum is 1. In the figure: 3 minus 3 plus 1 is 1.

76
00:12:29,427 --> 00:12:36,114
Now the proof. Let V be the union. We find how much one element v adds to the right side.

77
00:12:36,564 --> 00:12:48,546
Let T of v be the set of indices i with v in A sub i. v is in the union, so T of v is not empty. In the figure, v is in A 1 and A 3 only. So T of v is 1, 3.

78
00:12:48,996 --> 00:12:59,352
v is in A sub S exactly when v is in every A sub i with i in S. That means S is a nonempty subset of T of v.

79
00:12:59,802 --> 00:13:12,480
So v is counted in A 1, A 3, and A 1 3, with signs plus, plus, minus. The total is 1. In general, v adds the sum in Lemma 2.25, which is 1.

80
00:13:12,930 --> 00:13:25,654
Here is the whole proof in one table, for three small sets. Each row is an element v. Each column is a nonempty set S. A cell has the sign of S when v is in A sub S.

81
00:13:26,104 --> 00:13:32,838
The right side of the theorem adds the table column by column. Each column gives the size of A sub S with its sign.

82
00:13:33,288 --> 00:13:34,449
These add to 7.

83
00:13:34,899 --> 00:13:46,370
Now add the same table row by row. A row contains the sets S with S a nonempty subset of T of v. By Lemma 2.25, each row adds to 1.

84
00:13:46,820 --> 00:13:55,690
So the total is the number of rows, which is the size of V. The notes write this swap of the two sums in three lines. This completes the proof.

85
00:13:56,140 --> 00:14:05,706
Example 2.27 uses inclusion exclusion. Euler's function phi of n is the number of b from 1 to n with gcd of b and n equal to 1.

86
00:14:06,156 --> 00:14:16,930
Take n equals 12, which is 2 squared times 3. b shares a factor with 12 exactly when 2 or 3 divides b. A 1 is the 6 multiples of 2.

87
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:19,470
A 2 is the 4 multiples of 3.

88
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:30,369
A 1 intersect A 2 is the 2 multiples of 6. By inclusion exclusion, the union has 6 plus 4 minus 2, which is 8 elements.

89
00:14:30,819 --> 00:14:39,411
The other numbers are coprime to 12. phi of 12 is 12 minus 8, which is 4. They are 1, 5, 7 and 11.

90
00:14:39,861 --> 00:14:52,585
Now any n, with different primes p 1 to p m. b is in A sub S exactly when the product of the p sub i for i in S divides b. So the size of A sub S is n over that product.

91
00:14:53,035 --> 00:14:57,374
phi of n is n minus the size of the union. Put in inclusion exclusion.

92
00:14:57,824 --> 00:15:11,245
Move n into the sum as the term for the empty set. On the right, expand a product of two factors. Each term picks 1 or minus 1 over p i from each factor. S is the set of factors where we pick minus 1 over p i.

93
00:15:11,695 --> 00:15:22,933
So the sum is the expanded product. phi of n equals n times the product of 1 minus 1 over p i. For 12: 12 times one half times two thirds is 4.

94
00:15:23,383 --> 00:15:29,235
Try one more. Pause the video and find phi of 60. 60 is 2 squared times 3 times 5.

95
00:15:29,685 --> 00:15:39,530
The product formula gives 60 times one half times two thirds times four fifths. That is 16. The green cells are the 16 numbers coprime to 60.

96
00:15:39,980 --> 00:15:57,906
Inclusion exclusion gives the same number. Subtract the multiples of 2, 3 and 5: 30, 20 and 12. Add back the multiples of 6, 10 and 15: 10, 6 and 4. Subtract the multiples of 30: 2. The result is 16.

97
00:15:58,356 --> 00:16:08,619
To recap. The parts are the gaps, with end points 0 and n plus 1. The size is n plus 1, so M of x equals x times R of x.

98
00:16:09,069 --> 00:16:16,592
A gap condition is a condition on the middle parts. Use the Product Lemma for each length, and the Sum Lemma over all lengths.

99
00:16:17,042 --> 00:16:22,151
For assignment 3 question 1, a bijection that keeps the weight moves the problem to N cubed.

100
00:16:22,601 --> 00:16:34,379
In inclusion exclusion, each element adds exactly 1, by Lemma 2.25. It gives phi of n. The next video starts Chapter 3, binary strings.
